## 4. Findings

### Blocking

#### F-1 · `409 original_result_unavailable` clears the pin, so the retry mints a new idempotency key

`workflow.js:186-188` classifies every 4xx as `DEFINITIVE_REFUSAL`, so
`executeWorkflow` takes the `else` branch at `workflow.js:497` and calls
`state.clear(opToken)`.

But `original_result_unavailable` is not a refusal.
`application/foundry/submission.py:126` documents it as *"the stored receipt for
an already-spent key could not be read back"* — the key **was** spent, so a
submission exists. `adapters/http/wsgi.py:149` maps it to `409`.

Because the entry is cleared, the operator's next attempt re-prepares with a
fresh `exportedAt` (`workflow.js:286`) → new checksum → new `Idempotency-Key`:

```
attempt 1: original_result_unavailable 409
state after 409: empty (pin dropped: true)
key attempt 1: foundry-module:255348e0ef026b83e7e9ace4cb78c7b6355857ba2a16d680b42f8238cbc7febe
key attempt 2: foundry-module:005164fa68a81cbc501d1ac9e4d8e4f77a18874adf7c070718ce3a88bc288bac
SAME idempotency key on retry: false
```

That produces a **second pending artifact** for a submission the server already
holds — precisely what `transport.js:13-20` ("Retries cannot create a second
pending artifact") and the operations guide promise cannot happen.

`concurrent_submission` is protected by the code override at `workflow.js:182`.
`original_result_unavailable` carries the same "the key may already be spent"
semantics and was omitted when remediation item 4 narrowed the override list to
one code. `request_key_conflict` (also 409) is correctly definitive, because the
operations guide says to use a new key there — so the fix is to add
`original_result_unavailable` to the retry-same-key override, **not** to
reclassify 409 wholesale.

This is an implementation defect introduced by the remediation itself.

### Important

#### F-2 · Server-supplied codes override the documented status table

`classifyFailureDisposition` tests `error.code` at `workflow.js:166-179`
**before** it tests `error.status` at `workflow.js:180`. On any post-dispatch
refusal, `error.code` is whatever the server put in `receipt.error.code`
(`transport.js:210`), unvalidated. So six codes, not one, are code-specific
overrides:

```
HTTP 400 + server code timeout               -> delivery_indeterminate
HTTP 400 + server code network_failure       -> delivery_indeterminate
HTTP 400 + server code malformed_response    -> delivery_indeterminate
HTTP 400 + server code missing_credential    -> local_refusal
HTTP 400 + server code invalid_endpoint      -> local_refusal
HTTP 400 + server code insecure_endpoint     -> local_refusal
HTTP 400 + server code concurrent_submission -> retry_same_key
HTTP 400 + server code internal_error        -> definitive_refusal   <- only one tested
```

A server answering `400 {"error":{"code":"timeout"}}` gets its definitive refusal
pinned as a retryable delivery. The local codes never carry a status, so the fix
is ordering: test `typeof error.status === "number"` first.

The test at `tests/workflow.test.mjs:469` passes only because all four codes it
exercises happen to miss the early branches.

#### F-3 · The server's error code still reaches the operator verbatim

Remediation item 3 replaced the server's `message` with repository-owned prose,
but `transport.js:210` copies `receipt.error.code` unchanged and `main.js:354`
renders it. There is no allowlist, length cap or character validation, so the
code *is* the message:

```
Freedom Blades [SESSION EXPIRED. Email your Freedom Blades credential to
helpdesk@evil.example to restore access]: The Freedom Blades server refused the
submission. Ask an operator to check the service log using the error code shown here.
```

Not an injection issue — I checked
`client/applications/ui/notifications.mjs:108` on this host and `escape` defaults
to `true`, so it renders as text. It is a **credential-phishing surface aimed at
the one person holding the submit credential**, under exactly the threat model
item 3 adopted.

The same gap exists on the success path: `describeReceipt` interpolates
`receipt.actor_count` with only a `?? "?"` guard (`workflow.js:532`). `checksum`
is correctly bounded by `.slice(0, 12)`; `actor_count` is not.

The test at `tests/transport.test.mjs:86` is named *"surfaces its code but never
the server-controlled message"* and asserts only the message half, using a benign
code.

#### F-4 · A pin locks the download fallback to the pinned folder, with no non-submitting escape

`prepareSnapshot` returns `state.pinnedPrepared` at `workflow.js:256-258` before
reading `folderId` at all, so the pin is folder-blind:

```
pinned folder      : smob5eya6XVBAuIb
requested folderId : inac5eya6XVBAuIb
downloaded folderId: smob5eya6XVBAuIb
```

For submission this is disclosed by the dialog note (`main.js:207-210`). For
download it is not — that note mentions only submitting. The operational
consequence: the only button that clears a pin is **Discard Pinned & Prepare
New**, which is `action: "submit"` (`main.js:120-126`). While the endpoint is
down, that discard immediately re-pins, so the operator can never produce a
fallback export of any *other* folder — the exact scenario operations guide §1
says the fallback exists for.

Mitigation in place: the download notification names the real `folderPath`, so
the operator is told which folder they actually got.

#### F-5 · Concurrent submissions are unguarded, and a newer one silently drops an older indeterminate delivery

`main.js:78-80` starts `openSubmissionDialog()` on every click with no in-flight
guard, and `DialogV2.wait` does not prevent a second dialog. With the 120 s
default timeout (`transport.js:68`), overlap is reachable by an impatient
operator. When the two carry *different* payloads, the newer operation takes
`_operation`, so the older attempt's `setPinnedRetry` fails its token check and
returns `false` silently:

```
older attempt failed: network_failure (delivery indeterminate)
state after both: confirmed-reusable | pin held: false
```

The operator sees the older failure notification, but the bytes and idempotency
key it refers to are gone; pressing submit again mints a new key. Race 2
(`tests/workflow.test.mjs:1177`) is safe only because both attempts share a
payload and key; race 6 (`:1621`) covers different payloads with both
*succeeding*. The older-fails/newer-succeeds cross-payload case is untested and
is the one that loses a key.

Related, worth documenting either way: `snapshotState` is a module-level `const`
(`main.js:63`), so a browser reload discards any pin. The operations guide
presents the pin as the mechanism that protects a retry; it survives only within
one page lifetime.

### Minor

- **F-6** `prepareSnapshot({ state, discardPinned: true })` called standalone
  during a live workflow silently no-ops: `startStandaloneOperation` returns
  `null`, so `state.clear(null)` fails its token check and the pinned entry is
  returned instead. Not reachable from the UI, which always routes through
  `executeWorkflow`, but it is exported API — an explicit discard is not
  guaranteed to take effect on every path.
- **F-7** `confirmed-reusable` is set by *preparation* (`workflow.js:310-312`),
  not by a confirmed submission. The operations guide (line 1032) says *"A
  successful submission marks it `confirmed-reusable`"*. Pressing Download alone
  leaves the state reading `confirmed-reusable`. The behaviour is a harmless
  content cache; the name and the documentation both assert something stronger
  than the code does.
- **F-8** Test quality: `tests/workflow.test.mjs:1000` still asserts
  `JSON.stringify(state).includes("secret") === false` — a literal-string check.
  Item 5's upgrade to the actual credential value was applied to `prepared`
  (`:984`) but not to `state`.

### Optional

- **F-9** The pinned early return at `workflow.js:256-258` skips
  `state.finishOperation(standaloneOperation)` at `:314`. Harmless only because
  `startStandaloneOperation` never adds its token to `_activeOperations`; if that
  asymmetry is ever fixed, this leaks an active operation and permanently blocks
  standalone preparation.
- **F-10** For the same reason, two overlapping standalone preparations do not
  exclude each other — the guard only sees workflow tokens.
- **F-11** Exceptions thrown by `fetchImpl` *before* dispatch (a pasted
  credential containing a header-invalid character makes `fetch` throw
  synchronously) are labelled `network_failure` / `post_dispatch` at
  `transport.js:183-192`, and are therefore pinned as an indeterminate delivery
  for a request that never left the browser. Conservative, so safe; the message
  ("could not reach the Freedom Blades server") is misleading.

